        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          STS-108, which delivered the Expedition 4 crew to the Station and returned the Expedition 3 crew to Earth, was the 12th Shuttle mission to visit the International Space Station (ISS).  The crew consisted of Commander Dominic L. Gorie, Pilot Mark E. Kelly, Mission Specialists Linda M. Godwin and Daniel M. Tani, and the Expedition 4 crew Yuri I. Onufrienko, Carl E. Walz, and Daniel W. Bursch.  STS-108 was the fourth mission to utilize the Italian Multipurpose Logistics Module (MPLM), Raffaello.  The Raffaello MPLM was loaded with hardware, clothing, food, and supplies for use by the ISS crew.  Upon docking, Raffaello was attached to the Unity node of the Station while its cargo was unloaded.  Most of the cargo was transferred into the U.S. Destiny Laboratory Module, including eight resupply stowage racks and four resupply stowage platforms.  
<P>
Featured during STS-108 was a spacewalk to install thermal blankets over the Beta Gimbal Assemblies (BGAs) at the bases of the Space Station's solar panels. The BGAs, mounted atop the stations P6 Truss, control the panels to keep them at an optimal angle in relation to the sun. 
<P>
Life sciences experiments on this mission included several Detailed Supplementary Objective (DSO) experiments.  A DSO is a NASA-sponsored investigation performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers.  These studies are designed to require minimal crew time, power and stowage.  Biomedical DSOs focus on operational concerns, including space motion sickness, cardiovascular deconditioning, muscle loss, changes in coordination and balance strategies, radiation exposure, pharmacokinetics and changes in the body's biochemistry.
<P>
Other payloads on this mission included the Lightweight Mission Peculiar Support Structure Carrier (LMC) with four Get Away Special (GAS) canisters and the Multiple Application Customized Hitchhiker-1 (MACH-1) Payload. The MACH-1 payload contained the Starshine, Capillary Pumped Loop Experiment-3, the Prototype Synchrotron Radiation Detector, two Space Experiment Modules containing multiple small experiments and a GAS canister containing seven experiments from Utah State University.
<P>
Located in the middeck area were the Avian Development Facility and the Commercial Biomedical Testing Module Animal Enclosure Module. The Avian Development Facility was flown to validate subsystems and contained two experiments on the development of Japanese quail eggs in microgravity. The Animal Enclosure Module housed a commercial experiment that could lead to the discovery of better treatments for osteoporosis in humans. 
<P>
The STS-108 mission was also host to the "Flags for Heroes and Families" campaign.  Approximately 6,000 American flags flown on this mission were distributed to the victims' families and survivors of the September 11 terrorist attacks.  The STS-108 and Expedition 3 crews returned to Earth on December 17, 2001.
